const body = `<h1>5.1b — Your First Class: Write It Yourself</h1>

<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Basic Application</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h3>Build: Item Class</h3>
        <p>Yesterday you dissected the anatomy of a class. Today you'll write one from scratch. Create a class called <code>Item</code> (in its own file).</p>
        <ol>
            <li>Give it three instance variables: <code>String name</code>, <code>int weight</code>, <code>int value</code>.</li>
            <li>Make all instance variables <code>private</code>.</li>
            <li>Write a constructor that takes all three values.</li>
            <li>Write a <code>void display()</code> method that prints the item's stats in a formatted line.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h3>Test It</h3>
        <p>In a separate <code>ItemTester</code> class with a main method:</p>
        <ol>
            <li>Create three Item objects: "Sword" (weight 8, value 25), "Potion" (weight 1, value 10), "Shield" (weight 12, value 40).</li>
            <li>Call <code>display()</code> on each.</li>
            <li>What happens if you try to access <code>sword.name</code> directly from ItemTester? Why?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-05/class-design.html">Writing Classes</a></li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-05/constructors.html">Constructors</a></li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
